1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,522
Your universe works, and a crew can sit down and play it for an evening.

2
00:00:07,422 --> 00:00:10,900
This class is about the evening after that,

3
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:13,524
and the eighteen after that.

4
00:00:15,324 --> 00:00:20,976
Today you'll give your crew a question that takes twenty evenings to answer.

5
00:00:21,876 --> 00:00:24,920
And you'll find out, by measuring it,

6
00:00:25,370 --> 00:00:30,152
exactly what the game remembers from one week to the next.

7
00:00:31,352 --> 00:00:37,874
The people who make the game built a campaign on the same machinery you're using.

8
00:00:38,774 --> 00:00:40,513
It's called Storm's Beacon,

9
00:00:40,963 --> 00:00:45,311
and they wrote down what they decided as they went.

10
00:00:47,111 --> 00:00:50,154
First, every episode goes round one loop.

11
00:00:50,604 --> 00:00:55,387
Someone names a place, the crew goes, finds one thing out,

12
00:00:55,837 --> 00:00:58,011
gets interrupted, and reports back.

13
00:00:58,911 --> 00:01:02,389
Second, only three evenings are written by hand.

14
00:01:02,839 --> 00:01:05,448
The rest come from one pattern.

15
00:01:06,348 --> 00:01:10,696
Third, they built the pattern before they wrote the episodes.

16
00:01:12,496 --> 00:01:15,974
And they were honest about the hard part.

17
00:01:16,874 --> 00:01:18,613
It isn't the machinery.

18
00:01:19,063 --> 00:01:22,541
It's having enough for the crew to do.

19
00:01:23,741 --> 00:01:31,133
So let's look at what you have, the way a crew sees it on the first night.

20
00:01:32,033 --> 00:01:35,946
There are four leads, and all four are open.

21
00:01:37,746 --> 00:01:39,920
For one evening, that's generous.

22
00:01:40,370 --> 00:01:45,152
For a campaign, it's the whole map in the first minute.

23
00:01:46,052 --> 00:01:49,965
Three things change when the same people come back.

24
00:01:51,765 --> 00:01:55,243
You show them one lead at a time.

25
00:01:56,143 --> 00:01:59,622
A win belongs to the very last evening.

26
00:02:00,522 --> 00:02:05,304
And only what the game keeps is still true next week.

27
00:02:06,504 --> 00:02:10,417
The plan for a campaign isn't for the game,

28
00:02:10,867 --> 00:02:14,780
and it isn't for the crew. It's for you.

29
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:20,463
So it goes in a plain page beside the universe file.

30
00:02:22,263 --> 00:02:24,002
A premise: one question.

31
00:02:24,452 --> 00:02:27,496
Five boats and forty-one people are missing.

32
00:02:28,396 --> 00:02:31,439
A pressure that's true every single week.

33
00:02:31,889 --> 00:02:34,498
Somebody else is stripping those boats.

34
00:02:35,398 --> 00:02:38,007
And the loop, in five lines.

35
00:02:39,807 --> 00:02:41,980
Lint doesn't read this page,

36
00:02:42,430 --> 00:02:44,604
and neither does the game.

37
00:02:45,504 --> 00:02:50,722
I tried, with a page made to look exactly like a record.

38
00:02:51,922 --> 00:02:54,965
Now the part the game does read.

39
00:02:55,865 --> 00:02:58,909
One landmark, a lifeboat called the Wren.

40
00:02:59,359 --> 00:03:03,272
And one lead that sends the crew to her.

41
00:03:05,072 --> 00:03:09,420
You know every line of these from the last class.

42
00:03:10,320 --> 00:03:12,059
Two habits are new.

43
00:03:12,959 --> 00:03:16,002
The title starts with the campaign's name,

44
00:03:16,452 --> 00:03:21,235
because in week six the Quest Log is a long list.

45
00:03:22,135 --> 00:03:25,613
And the key starts with the evening's number,

46
00:03:26,063 --> 00:03:29,541
because you're going to write sixty of them.

47
00:03:30,741 --> 00:03:33,350
I save, and I run lint,

48
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:35,539
and it says clean.

49
00:03:37,339 --> 00:03:42,122
Now look at the count: one universe file, one story file.

50
00:03:43,022 --> 00:03:45,196
The campaign page isn't counted,

51
00:03:45,646 --> 00:03:48,254
which is exactly what we want.

52
00:03:49,454 --> 00:03:54,237
This next part is the measurement the whole class stands on.

53
00:03:55,137 --> 00:03:56,876
I pay the levy,

54
00:03:57,326 --> 00:03:59,065
I take a job,

55
00:03:59,515 --> 00:04:04,733
I follow the lead out to the Wren, and I come home.

56
00:04:06,533 --> 00:04:09,141
Then I write down six things.

57
00:04:09,591 --> 00:04:13,070
Where the ship is. The credits. The standing.

58
00:04:13,970 --> 00:04:15,709
The lead, the job,

59
00:04:16,159 --> 00:04:17,898
and the charted places.

60
00:04:19,698 --> 00:04:23,176
Now I close the game. All of it.

61
00:04:24,076 --> 00:04:27,120
And I type the same line again.

62
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:30,659
It's the same system,

63
00:04:31,109 --> 00:04:34,152
the same credits, and the same standing.

64
00:04:35,052 --> 00:04:37,226
The lead is still done,

65
00:04:37,676 --> 00:04:39,850
the job is still mine,

66
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:43,778
and the Wren is still on the chart.

67
00:04:44,978 --> 00:04:47,152
So that's a campaign's memory.

68
00:04:48,052 --> 00:04:49,791
Where the ship is,

69
00:04:50,241 --> 00:04:53,285
what the side has in the bank,

70
00:04:53,735 --> 00:04:57,213
and what every side thinks of this ship.

71
00:04:59,013 --> 00:05:01,187
Every step of the story,

72
00:05:01,637 --> 00:05:03,811
done, open, or still hidden.

73
00:05:04,711 --> 00:05:06,450
The jobs in hand,

74
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:09,074
and every place they've charted.

75
00:05:10,874 --> 00:05:13,917
That's enough to build twenty evenings on.

76
00:05:15,117 --> 00:05:17,726
And here's what it doesn't keep.

77
00:05:18,176 --> 00:05:19,915
Read this list twice.

78
00:05:21,715 --> 00:05:25,193
Guards the crew ran away from are back.

79
00:05:26,093 --> 00:05:27,833
A countdown starts again.

80
00:05:29,633 --> 00:05:34,850
A ship with a new name has no standing and no jobs,

81
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:39,213
and the old name's record is gone for good.

82
00:05:40,113 --> 00:05:44,461
A universe with a new title is a new game.

83
00:05:46,261 --> 00:05:48,000
And the big one.

84
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:53,683
The game has no memory for a fact of your story

85
00:05:54,133 --> 00:05:58,480
unless that fact is a step, a standing, or credits.

86
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:04,598
So in this class, every turn of the plot is a step.

87
00:06:05,798 --> 00:06:09,276
So you have a premise, a first lead,

88
00:06:09,726 --> 00:06:12,335
and a table you measured yourself.

89
00:06:14,135 --> 00:06:16,743
End every evening the same way:

90
00:06:17,193 --> 00:06:19,802
jump home, then close the game.

91
00:06:20,702 --> 00:06:25,485
Next time, we build a whole evening round that one lead.
